# =============================================================================
# numerus — Retired wrapped numeric type with a float marker (expect parse error).
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • A width marker is its own type — the wrapped form `numerus<W>` / `fractus<W>` (en `int<W>` / `float<W>`) is retired and rejected whole at parse time, whatever the marker, including a wrong-family one such as `int<f32>`; write the bare float marker (`f32`)
#   • Parse error — the diagnostic `PARSE040:numeric_wrapper_retired` names the form written and the bare replacement
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • writing the retired `numerus<f32>` for a floating-point type — the bare `f32` is the float spelling
#
# See also: numerus, fractus
# =============================================================================




# retired wrapped numeric type with a float marker (expect parse error)
#
# WHY: the wrapped form `numerus<W>` is retired; the bare `f32` is the float spelling.
#
# DELIBERATE: this exemplum keeps the retired spelling (`int<f32>`) on purpose, to
# pin the PARSE040 `numeric_wrapper_retired` diagnostic the parser gives every wrapped form (N7d).

main {
    const int<f32> invalid ← 1.0 ∷ f32
    print invalid
}